rachel: Support ticket — new user can't build the C# quickstart. They copied the snippet and `dotnet run` errors with 'The program does not contain a static 'Main' method suitable for an entry point'.

omar: which snippet? link?

rachel: quickstarts/dotnet/README.md. The example uses a top-level `await` call (like `var resp = await client.Responses.CreateAsync(..)` at file scope) but the project target in the csproj example is netcoreapp3.1. Top-level async main needs C# 9 / net5+. Users on older SDKs hit compile error.

jiayi: ah right. We unintentionally mixed modern top-level async code with an older TF in the csproj. Also noticed the sample shows `Install-Package Redwood` in the windows snippet but the actual NuGet is `Redwood.Inference`. New users copy the wrong package and get missing type errors.

omar: yikes, two separate copy/paste footguns. Any Windows-specific issues?

rachel: yep — the PowerShell snippet for setting env var uses bash syntax `export REDWOOD_API_KEY=...` in the same block as a PowerShell `dotnet run` command. Windows users copy/paste and don't set the env var. Support had to walk a user through `$env:REDWOOD_API_KEY = "sk_..."`.

jiayi: proposed short fixes: 1) update README to use an explicit async Main method (works on older TFs), 2) correct NuGet package name everywhere to `Redwood.Inference`, 3) add a csproj minimal `TargetFramework` example for net6.0 and note about C# version if using top-level await, 4) show both bash and PowerShell env var examples in separate labeled blocks.

omar: agree. What does an explicit Main look like?

jiayi: e.g.
```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Redwood.Inference;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REDWOOD_API_KEY");
        var client = new RedwoodClient(apiKey);
        var resp = await client.Responses.CreateAsync(new Request { Input = "hello" });
        Console.WriteLine(resp.Output);
    }
}
```
Add a `csproj` snippet: ` <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework> ` and a note "If you prefer top-level statements with await, target net6.0+ or use C#9+."

rachel: I'll also change the package install lines to `dotnet add package Redwood.Inference --version 1.*` and `Install-Package Redwood.Inference` for NuGet/Package Manager.

jiayi: and for Windows PowerShell env var: show `$env:REDWOOD_API_KEY = "sk_..."` and then `dotnet run` in the next block. For bash: `export REDWOOD_API_KEY=sk_...` then `dotnet run`.

omar: docs-bot: please remind about PR labeling and adding minimal SDK version.

docs-bot: Reminder: tag PR with docs/quickstart and sdk/dotnet. Add minimum supported SDK (.NET 6) and include compatibility note for older runtimes. Consider adding a CI snippet test to build the dotnet quickstart against net6.0 to detect future drift.

support-bot: linked ticket #20456 with user logs. They also attached the exact commands they ran on Windows.

jiayi: small additional UX nit: the website copy button sometimes copies the surrounding markdown fence with language hint and Windows users paste it into .cs file and get weird extras. Suggest including a plain "Copy C#" button or ensure copy excludes language hints.

omar: good call. I'll open a small follow-up to check the docs site copy behavior for fenced C# blocks.

rachel: PR #6123 opened updating the dotnet quickstart: replaces top-level await with explicit async Main, fixes NuGet package name to Redwood.Inference everywhere, adds csproj target sample for net6.0, and adds separate PowerShell and bash env var examples. cc @omar @jiayi for review.

jiayi: left a tiny wording nit about the apiKey variable name. LGTM after CI.

omar: merging once CI green. Also adding a checklist entry so language quickstarts include: correct package name, explicit build target, and platform-specific env var examples.

rachel: thanks all — hopefully fewer Windows support tickets now. :sparkles:
